= The Three Zones of General Knowledge

GK looks like an ocean, but it settles into three zones, and each zone has its own way of being learned. *Static GK* is the part that never changes — the books, the awards, the days, the firsts. *Current affairs* is the moving part — the last year's significant events. And *everyday knowledge* is the living part — the practical sense of how the world works. A topper's preparation treats the three zones differently instead of lumping them into one giant, scary pile.

- *Static GK rewards tables.* Organise facts by category, revise on a cycle, and the same facts serve you year after year.
- *Current affairs rewards a system.* A 15-minute daily habit with a note grid beats a 15-hour cram before the exam — memory is built by repeated small exposures.
- *Everyday knowledge rewards curiosity.* Every "why does that happen?" you actually look up becomes a question you can answer in the hall.
- *The zones overlap on purpose.* "Chandrayaan-3 landed near the Moon's south pole" is a current event whose STATIC hooks (ISRO, the Moon, India's space firsts) live in your static tables.
- *One notebook rules all three zones:* the 5-field note grid — what, when, where/who, why it matters, static link.

#analogy-box(title: "Real-World Analogy")[
  Think of GK preparation like *maintaining a garden*. Static GK is the permanent planting — the trees you set once and water on schedule (your tables). Current affairs is the seasonal bed — new plants every month, old ones pruned when they stop being news. Everyday knowledge is the wild part that grows if you simply keep looking at it with interest. No gardener does it all in one weekend; the garden rewards a *little care every day*.
]

= Static GK Map 1 — Books, Authors and Awards

Two categories where the facts never rot: who wrote what, and which award honours what. The trick is not reading long lists — it is *attaching one hook to each fact*. A hook is a single memory string: Tagore — first Indian Nobel — *Gitanjali*. Kalam — missile man, President — *Wings of Fire*. One hook per fact, and the pair survives for years.

