= The Currency Check — Your Last-Mile Verification

Static facts from your tables are safe forever, but a small set of facts has a shelf life: officeholders, award winners, and anything named after a living tradition. These are exactly the facts exam papers recycle year after year, so a short verification pass in your final revision week protects every current-affairs mark you prepared for.

- *Who holds it now?* Re-verify the President, Vice President, Prime Minister, and the governors or chief ministers you have noted in grids.
- *Who won it this year?* The current year's major award winners — literature, peace, cinema, sports — from one consolidated list.
- *What is it called now?* Award and scheme names change; the Khel Ratna renaming is the standing example of why names need a currency check.
- *What happened in the last quarter?* Skim your most recent note grids first — recent events fade fastest and are asked most.
- *One source, one hour.* Do this check from a single reliable current source in one sitting — scattered checks miss things.

